  "response": "The question of what is lost and what is gained in burning was, for the better part of a century, a dispute about the direction of a transfer. Georg Ernst Stahl formulated the phlogiston theory in the early eighteenth century, and on his account combustion was a giving off: a burning body released a material substance called phlogiston into the air. Whereas that doctrine made the burning body the donor, Jean Rey had argued in 1630 that calcined tin gained weight by taking up matter from the air, and it was only after Stahl's system had become the common property of chemists that this contrary suggestion was seriously revived. Rey's conjecture, in any case, preceded by nearly a century and a half the experiment that would decide between the two accounts.\n\nLavoisier heated tin in a hermetically sealed glass retort. There was no change in the total mass of the sealed retort after the metal had been calcined inside it. Lavoisier stated the principle that the total mass of matter is conserved in a chemical change, offering it as a general axiom of his chemistry. The balance had more to tell. When Lavoisier broke the seal of the retort after heating, air rushed into the vessel, and that inrush shows that the weight gained by the metal equalled the weight of air lost from the sealed vessel.\n\nA difficulty attends the usual description of this work. Lavoisier's calcination trials were carried out in open crucibles standing in the free atmosphere; but a crucible standing open to the surrounding air cannot be squared with the heating of the tin in a hermetically sealed glass retort, since one and the same trial cannot have been both closed against the air and exposed to it.\n\nJoseph Priestley obtained a new gas in 1774 by heating mercuric oxide with a burning lens, and as a result he called that gas dephlogisticated air. Henry Cavendish discovered oxygen in 1766 by heating red calx of mercury, and this rules out the first procurement of the gas from the mercury calx having come eight years later at Priestley's hands: the discovery cannot belong to Cavendish in 1766 and also be the work of 1774.\n\nThe outcome admits of just two descriptions, of which exactly one must hold and not both. Either phlogiston theory was ultimately retained as the accepted account of combustion, or \u2014 even though the gas drawn from mercuric oxide was christened dephlogisticated air, in the very idiom of the older doctrine \u2014 phlogiston theory was ultimately abandoned in favour of the oxygen theory of combustion. The weighings in closed vessels, rather than any single polemic, are what made the choice between them unavoidable.",
